  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will continue to bring pleasant weather through \nSaturday, with clear skies and seasonable temperatures. With \nnights starting to get a little longer and some boundary layer \nmoisture in place, areas of valley fog will develop in the \novernight hours, lasting through sunrise. Daily high \ntemperatures expected to be 80s today through Sunday. Due to \ngood radiational cooling expected tonight, we have blended the \nNBM10 guidance into the minimum temperature guidance to account \nfor more cooling than an average forecast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA broad upper level trough will build over Canada during the \nfirst half of next week with seasonable to slightly below \naverage temperatures and higher chances for precipitation. CAPE \nand shear values increase and combine from Sunday afternoon \nthrough Tuesday enough to flag that time period for thunderstorm\nactivity. The most active day appears to be Tuesday as low \npressure brings a frontal system across the area. Convective \nparameters look weaker than recent setups that produced severe \nweather in our region, but the trends bare watching closely. The\nbroad trough will keep a risk of showers going through the end \nof the week.",
